1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,112
Aziz orders a bookshelf in the marketplace app, and it must come to his flat.

2
00:00:05,162 --> 00:00:07,032
He taps Add address.

3
00:00:07,082 --> 00:00:12,096
The platform already knows his registered address, because MyID gave it at his proofing.

4
00:00:12,146 --> 00:00:17,280
The marketplace is an app of the same bank, on the same phone, for the same person.

5
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:21,432
So why should anyone ask Aziz before the marketplace gets that address?

6
00:00:21,482 --> 00:00:26,280
In Lesson 27, Aziz linked his marketplace account in the mobile bank.

7
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:31,656
This lesson is the first time that the marketplace wants something from his verified identity.

8
00:00:31,706 --> 00:00:34,560
Start with what the marketplace can normally see.

9
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:39,480
Each app has its own sessions, and its backend accepts only its own tokens.

10
00:00:39,530 --> 00:00:41,352
Lesson 5 showed that wall.

11
00:00:41,402 --> 00:00:48,888
Through it, the marketplace gets a short summary of the customer: the level, the verified phone number, and a display name.

12
00:00:48,938 --> 00:00:51,336
The summary never holds an address.

13
00:00:51,386 --> 00:00:55,728
The address sits in the protected part of the record, with the passport data.

14
00:00:55,778 --> 00:00:59,520
The bank's keys encrypt it, and every read of it is audited.

15
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:05,400
So there is exactly one way for an address to leave: a sharing request that Aziz approves.

16
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:07,272
Here is how it runs.

17
00:01:07,322 --> 00:01:13,056
The marketplace backend asks the platform for a sharing request, with its own marketplace token.

18
00:01:13,106 --> 00:01:15,576
The platform first checks two things.

19
00:01:15,626 --> 00:01:17,448
Is Aziz at level two?

20
00:01:17,498 --> 00:01:20,040
And is his marketplace account linked?

21
00:01:20,090 --> 00:01:23,592
If either answer is no, the platform does not ask him.

22
00:01:23,642 --> 00:01:28,080
It tells the marketplace to send Aziz to the mobile bank first.

23
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:33,432
Aziz is at level two, and his account is linked, so the request goes on.

24
00:01:33,482 --> 00:01:38,808
The platform keeps it for the configured waiting time, and sends a push signal to the mobile bank.

25
00:01:38,858 --> 00:01:42,552
Again, the push carries only the request identifier.

26
00:01:42,602 --> 00:01:46,368
The mobile bank pulls the request, and asks one question.

27
00:01:46,418 --> 00:01:49,128
Share your verified address with the marketplace?

28
00:01:49,178 --> 00:01:50,760
Aziz taps Share.

29
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:57,000
Now the platform reads the address from the protected record, for one purpose: address sharing.

30
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:02,184
It writes an audit event that names the reader, the marketplace, and the purpose.

31
00:02:02,234 --> 00:02:06,312
The marketplace collects the address one time, and saves it.

32
00:02:06,362 --> 00:02:08,064
The bookshelf has a place to go.

33
00:02:08,114 --> 00:02:09,936
Now the other answers.

34
00:02:09,986 --> 00:02:15,576
If Aziz taps Refuse, nothing is saved, and the marketplace gets a refusal.

35
00:02:15,626 --> 00:02:21,216
If he does not answer in the configured time, the result is the same: nothing is saved.

36
00:02:21,266 --> 00:02:23,952
The audit trail records the refusal too.

37
00:02:24,002 --> 00:02:29,544
Next month, Aziz adds his address once more, this time on the marketplace website.

38
00:02:29,594 --> 00:02:31,464
The mobile bank asks again.

39
00:02:31,514 --> 00:02:35,904
There is no standing consent: one answer covers one address, one time.

40
00:02:35,954 --> 00:02:39,336
I think this is the right choice, even if it costs one more tap.

41
00:02:39,386 --> 00:02:49,680
A box that Aziz ticked once, long ago, would let the marketplace read his address forever, after every move, without him ever seeing it again.

42
00:02:49,730 --> 00:02:55,056
A fresh question each time keeps the decision with Aziz, at the moment it matters.

43
00:02:55,106 --> 00:02:56,928
And when he moves out of that flat?

44
00:02:56,978 --> 00:03:00,720
Aziz deletes the saved address in the marketplace itself.

45
00:03:00,770 --> 00:03:03,552
The platform does not take part in that deletion.

46
00:03:03,602 --> 00:03:09,456
Once shared, the copy belongs to the marketplace, and the marketplace must let him remove it.

47
00:03:09,506 --> 00:03:11,496
Look at what this design does not do.

48
00:03:11,546 --> 00:03:15,048
The marketplace never holds a key to the identity record.

49
00:03:15,098 --> 00:03:17,352
It never reads the passport data.

50
00:03:17,402 --> 00:03:20,664
It cannot ask for the address on a session at level one.

51
00:03:20,714 --> 00:03:26,400
And it cannot skip the question, because the question lives in the mobile bank, not in the marketplace.

52
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:31,536
To recap: the address leaves the protected record only through a sharing request.

53
00:03:31,586 --> 00:03:34,584
The request needs level two and a linked account.

54
00:03:34,634 --> 00:03:42,600
The mobile bank asks each time, the platform audits each read, and the customer deletes saved addresses in the marketplace.

55
00:03:42,650 --> 00:03:49,944
Aziz now has many sessions: the mobile bank on his phone, the marketplace app, and a browser at his parents' house.

56
00:03:49,994 --> 00:03:56,928
Suppose a stranger gets a session of his own in some other browser, and tries to end Aziz's session in the mobile bank.

57
00:03:56,978 --> 00:03:58,320
Which session wins?

58
00:03:58,370 --> 00:04:00,912
Next: sessions and their rank.
